  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">South Programme</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="3">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">In India and Bangladesh the target groups are poor and marginalised people, particularly women, landless and small-scale farmers, minorities and indigenous peoples. Youth and children have recently been included to a greater extent because they are seen as a key to sustainable development. A rights-based approach also entails working to influence policy makers and other relevant duty-bearers, such as governmental authorities and institutions.</narrative>
  </description>
  <description type="2">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Outcome 1: Strengthened ability of community-based organisations to claim their human rights, particularly on issues related to gender equality, food sovereignty and environment, to participate in public spheres and decision-making processes and to work independently for social change and accountable governance. 

Outcome 2: Decreased gender and caste-based violence through improved capacity of women and girls to take action in claiming their rights and promoting gender equality, as well as raised awareness among men and boys about gender inequality and its impacts. 

Outcome 3: Increased food sovereignty and climate resilience for marginalised communities through adoption of bio-diverse, climate-resilient and sustainable agriculture and natural resource management practices. 

Outcome 4: Increased sustainable livelihoods for marginalised communities through skills training, market linkages, access to and control over land and natural resources, as well as access to relevant government schemes and resources.  

Outcome 5: Strengthened local civil society organisations with increased capacity to network with other civil society organisations, to dialogue with duty-bearers and to effectively implement their work within the areas of PMERL, governance, fundraising, HRBA, gender mainstreaming, environment- and conflict sensitivity.</narrative>
  </description>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">The South Programme has an overall goal to empower marginalised communities in India and Bangladesh to claim their rights to gender equality, food sovereignty and ecologically sensitive, climate-resilient and sustainable livelihoods through a strengthened civil society at community and partner level. The project is funded by Sida and implemented together with 6 local partner organisations in India and Bangladesh. It will run until December 2028.</narrative>
  </description>
